$ Milestone 6 ("for lowers against the internal FlyIterator protocol",
$ docs/architecture.md §3.4/§4.1's runtime/iter.c): `for x in <iterable>`
$ over each of the three iterable tags -- coll (elements), board (keys, per
$ iter.c's documented design choice), and tex (one-byte substrings) -- plus
$ an early `give` from inside a `for` body to exercise the iterator's
$ cleanup-on-early-return path (see codegen.cpp's iterStack_/releaseAllScopes).

$ 1. coll: iterates elements in order.
nums = [10, 20, 30, 40]
total = 0
for n in nums {
    total = total + n
}
show(total)

$ 2. board: iterates keys (insertion order); index back into the board
$ inside the loop to get each value.
ages = {"amy": 30, "ben": 25, "cleo": 41}
for name in ages {
    show(name)
    show(ages[name])
}

$ 3. tex: iterates one-byte substrings.
for ch in "fly" {
    show(ch)
}

$ 4. Nested for loops.
grid = [[1, 2], [3, 4]]
for row in grid {
    for cell in row {
        show(cell)
    }
}

$ 5. Early `give` from inside a `for` loop (inside a job), proving the
$ iterator is cleaned up correctly on that path too, not just on normal
$ loop exhaustion.
job firstAbove(coll, threshold) {
    for v in coll {
        if v > threshold {
            give v
        }
    }
    give -1
}
show(firstAbove([1, 4, 9, 16, 25], 10))
show(firstAbove([1, 2, 3], 10))

$ 6. An empty coll: the loop body must not run at all.
for x in [] {
    show("should never print")
}
show("done")
